\chapter{Tutorial: Reviewing papers in the TUI}
\label{ch:tutorial-reviewing-in-the-tui}

[[reviewing-in-the-tui]] teaches the second half of a review: turning a
list of hits into decisions.
Its object of learning is that a decision is a small record---a status
(kept or discarded), the tags that motivate or theme it, and who made
it---and that the terminal interface of \cref{tui-module} is merely one
way to write that record.
The lesson therefore never asks the learner to look at the screen and
believe it; every TUI step is followed by a command that reads the saved
session back, so what the learner verifies is the record, not the frame.

The arc keeps the session invariant and varies the decision:
\begin{enumerate}
\item create one session with five papers (step 1), the material every
  later step works on;
\item make the simplest possible decision, a quick keep, and read it back
  (steps 2--3);
\item make the two decisions that need a tag, a discard with a motivation
  and a keep with a theme, and read them back (step 4);
\item see the theme surface outside the session (step 5) and the whole
  record leave the tool as a file (step 6);
\item name the rule the tags taught (steps 7--8).
\end{enumerate}
This is variation theory's contrast pattern applied to the decision
record: quick keep, keep with theme and discard share the same shape and
the same key-then-read-back rhythm, and differ in exactly one aspect
each, so the learner can see which part of the record each key writes.

A word on validation.
pytorial checks the transcript of each shell step, and a full-screen
Textual application draws itself with escape sequences that are not
stable text.
The TUI steps are therefore anchored on the command the learner types and
on the one line Scholar prints before the interface starts; the proof
that the keys did their work is delegated to the [[sessions show]] steps
that follow, whose output is plain text.
That is also the pedagogical point: the interface is transient, the
record is what remains.

