\section{Step 1: Create the session to review}

The learner runs the seeding search themselves rather than having a
[[pre_command]] do it.
Partly this is continuity with the first tutorial
(\cref{ch:tutorial-first-search}), where [[-n]] was the last thing
learned; partly it is a property of the runner: a hook runs as a plain
subprocess of the [[scholar]] process, so it would not be the same
command the learner later types by hand.
The query and provider are the same as in the first tutorial so the
search is served from the shared cache and the five papers are the ones
the learner has already seen.
The anchor is Scholar's own confirmation line, printed on standard
error, together with the pending count; the session name is what every
later step refers to.

Because the tutorial shell keeps Scholar's data directory inside the
tutorial's workspace, this session cannot collide with one of the
learner's real sessions of the same name, and [[--restart]] on the
tutorial discards it together with the workspace.

<<seed session step>>=
# Create the session to review

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-n\s+tui-review\b
  - "Saved session:"
  - 5 pending
hint: "Search OpenAlex for literate programming, five results, and persist the search under the session name `tui-review` with the `-n` option."
```

Create the session this lesson reviews:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5 -n tui-review
```

Scholar reports where the session was saved and that all five papers
are pending. Pending means no decision has been recorded yet; that is
what the rest of this lesson changes.

The tutorial shell has its own Scholar data directory, so this session
lives only inside the tutorial and never appears in your real session
list.
@

\section{Step 2: Keep a paper with one key}

The first decision is the smallest one the interface offers: [[K]] keeps
the highlighted paper without asking anything.
Starting with the quick keep separates the \emph{status} part of a
decision from the \emph{tags} part; step 4 then adds tags while keeping
the key-press shape the same, so the learner meets the two parts of the
record one at a time.

The required patterns are the [[resume]] command and the
\enquote{Resuming session} line Scholar prints unconditionally before
the interface takes over the terminal.
Nothing from inside the interface is matched: Textual redraws the screen
with control sequences, and matching on them would make the step fail
for reasons unrelated to the learner's actions.
The prose tells the learner to quit with [[q]] explicitly, because the
session is saved only when the interface exits, and step 3 depends on
that.

<<quick keep step>>=
# Keep a paper with one key

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+resume\s+tui-review\b
  - "Resuming session"
hint: "Open the saved session in the review interface with `sessions resume`, press the capital-K key once, then quit with `q`."
```

Open the session in the review interface:

```
scholar sessions resume tui-review
```

The interface lists the five papers with the first one highlighted.
Move with `j` and `k` (or the arrow keys); `Enter` opens a paper's
abstract and details, and `q` (or `Escape`) returns to the list.

For this step:

1. Leave the first paper highlighted and press `K` (capital K). This
   marks it as kept, with no further questions.
2. Press `q` to quit the interface.

The session is written to disk when the interface closes. The next step
reads it back.
@

\section{Step 3: Read the decision back}

This step is the other half of step 2 and the pattern the rest of the
lesson repeats: a key in the interface, then [[sessions show]] on the
command line.
The anchor [[Kept (1)]] is the heading Scholar prints for the kept
group, with its count; it can only appear if the [[K]] of step 2 was
saved, so this is where the previous step is actually verified.
The prose points out that the same title now sits under a different
heading, so the learner connects the key press to a change in the
record rather than to a change on the screen.

<<read back keep step>>=
# Read the decision back

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+show\s+tui-review\b
  - "Kept (1)"
  - "Pending (4)"
hint: "Print the session with `sessions show`; the paper you kept now appears under its own heading."
```

Look at what the interface saved:

```
scholar sessions show tui-review
```

The paper you pressed `K` on has moved from `Pending` to `Kept (1)`,
and four papers remain pending. This listing is the review record; the
interface is only one way to write it.
@

\section{Step 4: Discard with a motivation, keep with a theme}

Now the learner makes the two decisions that carry tags, in one visit
to the interface, so the modal that collects tags is seen twice in a
row with one difference: for a discard it refuses to close without a
motivation, for a keep with themes it does not.
That difference is the invariant the tutorial is after---a discard is
a claim that needs a reason, and the tool enforces it.
The tag names are prescribed ([[off-topic-false-hit]],
[[foundations]]) so that the read-back can anchor on them; they are also
the vocabulary the later tutorials reuse, so the learner meets them here
first.

The instructions spell out the modal's keys because the modal is the
one place where the interface is not self-explanatory: with no
existing tags the input field has focus, typing a name and pressing
[[Enter]] adds and selects it, and a second [[Enter]] on the empty
field confirms.
The read-back anchors on the two group headings and on the theme in
brackets after the kept paper, which is how [[sessions show]] displays
a tag.

<<discard and theme step>>=
# Discard with a motivation, keep with a theme

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+resume\s+tui-review\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+show\s+tui-review\b
  - "Discarded (1)"
  - "Kept (2)"
  - "[foundations]"
  - "[off-topic-false-hit]"
hint: "Resume the session, press `d` on one pending paper and give the motivation `off-topic-false-hit`, press `T` on another and give the theme `foundations`, quit, then print the session with `sessions show`."
```

Resume the session again:

```
scholar sessions resume tui-review
```

Now record two decisions that need a tag:

1. Move to a pending paper and press `d` to discard it. A dialog asks
   for a motivation. Type `off-topic-false-hit` and press `Enter` to
   add it, then press `Enter` again to confirm. The dialog will not
   close without at least one motivation: a discard needs a reason.
2. Move to another pending paper and press `T` to keep it with a
   theme. The same kind of dialog asks for themes. Type `foundations`,
   press `Enter` to add it and `Enter` again to confirm.
3. Press `q` to quit.

Then read the record back:

```
scholar sessions show tui-review
```

The listing now has a `Discarded (1)` group and `Kept (2)`, and the
tags appear in brackets after the titles. `K`, `T` and `d` all write
the same kind of record; they differ only in the status they set and in
whether they ask for tags.
@

\section{Step 5: See the theme outside the session}

Themes are not private to a session: [[scholar themes list]] counts
them across every saved session, which is what makes them a vocabulary
rather than a note.
Placing this step right after the theme was created gives the contrast
its shortest possible distance: the same word, typed once in a modal,
now appears in a table that mentions no session.
The anchor is the theme name and the table title.

<<themes list step>>=
# See the theme outside the session

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+themes\s+list\b
  - "Themes Across Sessions"
  - foundations
hint: "The `themes` command group has a `list` verb that counts themes over all saved sessions."
```

List the themes Scholar knows about:

```
scholar themes list
```

The theme you typed in the dialog is now listed with the number of
papers that carry it. Themes are shared across sessions, so the same
vocabulary can organise several reviews; `scholar themes show
foundations` would list the papers behind the count.
@

\section{Step 6: Export the record}

The last shell step takes the record out of Scholar.
The CSV export is chosen over the LaTeX report because its first line
is a header that names the fields of a decision---[[status]],
[[title]], \ldots, [[tags]]---so [[head -1]] shows the learner the
shape of the record they have been writing, column by column.
The anchors are the export confirmation and the start of that header
line.

<<export csv step>>=
# Export the record

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+tui-review\s+.*-f\s+csv\b
  - "Exported:"
  - "status,title"
hint: "Use `sessions export` with `-f csv` and `-o tui-review`, then show the first line of the resulting file with `head -1`."
```

Write the session to a file:

```
scholar sessions export tui-review -f csv -o tui-review
head -1 tui-review.csv
```

The first line of the file is the header: one column per part of a
decision (`status`, the paper's metadata, `tags`, who decided, and a
`paper_id` you can use to address a paper from a script). Every row
below it is one decision, exactly as the interface saved it. The
`latex` format writes a compilable report instead; `-f all` writes
both.
@

\section{Step 7: What a discard needs}

The first question step names the rule that step 4 enforced silently.
It is asked \emph{after} the learner has felt the dialog refuse to
close, so the answer is a recollection rather than a guess; the two
distractors are things the interface also offers (notes, PDFs) but
never requires.

<<discard rule question step>>=
# What a discard needs

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - A note explaining the decision
  - At least one motivation tag
  - A downloaded PDF
answers:
  - At least one motivation tag
hint: "Think about what the dialog refused to close without when you pressed `d`."
```

Which of these must a discarded paper have before Scholar records the
decision?
@

\section{Step 8: Which keys write a decision}

The closing question fuses the lesson: of the keys the learner has seen
in the interface, which ones change the record?
[[n]] and [[s]] are the distractors---both were mentioned in the steps,
and both act on the interface or on notes without setting a status---so
choosing correctly means distinguishing writing a decision from
everything else the interface can do.

<<decision keys question step>>=
# Which keys write a decision

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - K
  - T
  - d
  - n
  - s
answers:
  - K
  - T
  - d
hint: "Pick the keys that change a paper's status; leave out the ones that edit notes or reorder the list."
```

Select every key in the review interface that records a keep or a
discard.
@
